# Billing rules

## Money

- `Charge.pence` is an integer. No `Money` class, no decimal.js — tried in 2024,
  reverted: precision only matters at the single `Math.round` ending a charge line.
- `Tariff.rate` is a float on purpose (`NUMERIC` in `002_tariffs.sql`); real rates
  are fractional, e.g. 28.47 p/kWh. Round the product, never the inputs.
- Nothing in `src/domain` emits pounds, and `getCharge` returns the pence verbatim.
  An API field named `*_pounds` is a bug unless the API layer divides, for display.
- `Tariff` has no unit field: `kind` decides whether `rate` is p/kWh or p/kVA/day,
  so nothing in the types catches a capacity rate handed to the energy path.

## Rounding order

- Sum in Wh, convert to kWh once, then apply the rate. Rounding per reading drifts by pounds over a quarter.
- One `Math.round` per charge line, at the very end. Fixed order, not taste:
  ADR 001 traces three rounding bugs to converting more than once.
- `Math.round` is half-up toward +∞, so it is asymmetric across zero:
  `Math.round(-2.5) === -2`. Fine for charges, wrong for credit notes.

Worked example — one quarter, fixed tariff, 4368 readings at 28.47 p/kWh:

```
sumWh      -> 1284617 Wh       exact, BIGINT
toKwh      -> 1284.617 kWh     one conversion
x rate     -> 36573.04599 p
Math.round -> 36573 p          = £365.73
```

Rounded per line instead, the Q3 2024 run drifted £9.12 over one portfolio.

## Standing charges

- Per site per day, every day in the window, reading or no reading.
- Day count comes from `daysBetween` on the window bounds — never from distinct
  buckets, or a comms outage turns into a silent discount.
- Its own charge line, rounded separately; folded into the energy line before
  rounding it produces a customer query every single time.
- `capacity` tariffs are pence per kVA per day: a standing charge in all but
  name, multiplied by `sites.capacity_kva`, never by kWh.

## VAT

- Every pence figure in `src/domain` is net. A VAT constant in the domain is a bug.
- Domestic 5%, business 20%. The split belongs to the customer account, not the
  site and not the tariff; billing does not know it and must not learn it.
- VAT applies to the invoice total after all net lines are summed, never per
  line. The two differ by a penny or two and HMRC cares which you used.

## Credit notes

- A credit note is a negative-pence charge: re-run `chargeFor` over the corrected
  period and subtract the original. Never mutate or delete an issued charge.
- Round each side as a positive integer and subtract the integers — rounding a
  negative product directly leaks a penny to the customer about half the time.
- A correction is never a negative reading; `toReading` rejects `value < 0`.

## Re-billing after a meter correction

- A correction replaces buckets we already hold; `readings` is keyed on
  `(site_id, bucket)`, so it has to be an upsert. `insertReadings` still issues a
  bare `INSERT`, so today a correction collides with the primary key instead.
- Re-bill the whole settlement window, not the corrected buckets. Partial
  re-bills caused the March 2025 double-charge: two corrections in one window
  each produced a delta against the original invoice and the overlap billed twice.
- Always a credit note for the original plus a fresh charge; no adjust-in-place.

## Estimated vs actual reads

- Estimates are ordinary rows; there is no flag on `readings`, and the ingest job
  records no provenance either. Adding a column for it needs an ADR.
- An estimated read is billable; four consecutive estimated quarters is not.
- An actual read arriving after an estimate is just a meter correction — same
  upsert, same full-window re-bill, same credit note. Do not special-case it.

## The settlement window

- Half-open, `[from, to)`, matching `readingsForSite`. The last bucket of a
  quarter belongs to the next window; every off-by-one began as an inclusive end.
- UTC only (`lib/dates.ts`). A standing-charge day is 24 hours, so clock-change
  days bill 23 or 25 hours of energy against one day of charge. Correct; queried twice.
- Tariff selection uses the window's `to` instant, so a tariff starting mid-window
  applies to the whole window. Split windows are not implemented; bill two windows.
- An empty window is a £0 charge, not an error; a missing tariff is an error —
  `chargeFor` throws `DomainError("no_tariff")` rather than reusing an expired rate.

## Known divergences — intended rule above, actual code below

| Rule | What the code does |
| --- | --- |
| ADR 002 precedence `capacity` > `timeOfUse` > `fixed` | `chargeFor` takes `active[0]`; silently undercharges on switch days |
| Capacity is pence per kVA per day | `chargeFor` does `kwh * rate` for every kind; `capacity_kva` never reaches it |
| Standing charges, VAT, credit notes | not implemented at all |
| `toKwh` only at a presentation boundary (ADR 001) | `billing.ts` calls it inside the domain |
| Corrections upsert on `(site_id, bucket)` | `insertReadings` is a plain `INSERT` with no `ON CONFLICT` |
